#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079517} :activity/id #uuid "6c809259-8a13-5c78-bd80-d0d6e8fe9b1b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820 :topic/id #uuid "c2c914b7-9441-5dec-a44d-57eb7fa8577a" :topic/math-academy-id 673} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079656 :tutorial/content "When writing out a sum, we often have to write a lot of terms. For example, the following sum has five terms:\n\n$$\nS = 2+4+6+8+10\n$$\n\nWe could just as easily encounter a sum with ten terms, or a hundred terms, or a thousand terms, or even more!\n\nThankfully, there is a notation called **sigma notation** that allows us to write sums more compactly, without writing every term.\n\nTo express the given sum $S$ using sigma notation, we first need to come up with the general term $a_n$ in our sum. Here, the $n$th term of our sum has the form $a_n = 2n,$ where $n$ ranges from ${\\color{red}1}$ to ${\\color{blue}5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)=2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)=4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2(3)=6 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2(4)=8 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =2(5)=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our sum can be written as\n\n$$\nS = a_{\\color{red}1} + a_2 + a_3 + a_4 + a_{\\color{blue}5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we use the Greek letter $\\displaystyle\\sum$ (called \"sigma\") to denote our summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n\\,=\\,1}^{5}({a}_{n})={\\sum }_{n\\,=\\,1}^{5}(2n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe write the $n$th term $({\\color{black}a_n=2n})$ to the right of $\\displaystyle\\sum,$ we put the first index $({\\color{red}1})$ below the sigma as $n={\\color{red}1},$ and we put the last index $({\\color{blue}5})$ above the sigma.\n\nWe can retrieve the expanded sum by substituting all values between $\\color{red}1$ and $\\color{blue}5$ into the $n$th term, and separating each result with a \"$+$\" sign, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}(2n) & =2(1)+2(2)+2(3)+2(4)+2(5) \\\\  & =2+4+6+8+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The sum\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{5} 2n = 2+4+6+8+10\n$$\n\nis an example of a **series**. A series is what we get when we add the terms of a *sequence* together. In this case, we're summing the terms of the sequence $a_n = 2n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "27542f45-7f00-500f-8ef4-8f36f6f8b957" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2837 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0d627eef-ef65-5238-9901-90c3400ac3c8" :step/math-academy-id 486637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078569 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078289 :question/id #uuid "be3fc348-da73-5858-bea0-6023bf044e11" :question/math-academy-id "e-4169"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "497072ab-d582-5896-b8a2-12bf1f07c8e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532712}] :knowledge-point/title "Expanding Sums Written in Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "64d8a6fd-3407-5153-8d34-5325bd887b5a" :step/math-academy-id 486638 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078570 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078287 :question/id #uuid "50074a6d-1ca5-580b-a716-0a69bcae773d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4166"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc0a5e10-c402-54cb-80e6-ba4fcc3e9df4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549960}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Written in Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "366dea1e-1586-59cc-a106-0ebd1a1da354" :step/math-academy-id 486641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079610 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to express the following series in sigma notation.\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1}, \\quad {\\color{red}2}, \\quad {\\color{red}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{red}19}, \\quad {\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $20$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}a_n} = {\\color{red}n}, \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20.\n$$\n\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad {\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{blue}20}, \\quad {\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nSince the value in the denominator is always *one more than* the numerator, the $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}b_n} = {\\color{blue}n + 1} , \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}a_n}{\\color{blue}b_n} =\\dfrac{\\color{red}n}{\\color{blue}n+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=20.$ \n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}\\frac{n}{n+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by writing out the summation in full and checking that we recover the original sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}\\frac{n}{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+1}+\\frac{2}{2+1}+⋯+\\frac{19}{19+1}+\\frac{20}{20+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}+⋯+\\frac{19}{20}+\\frac{20}{21}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0c45a9c6-5ee9-5a89-8398-6a3eb37f5f63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13578 :tutorial/title "Expressing Sums in Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "4949bbd4-b355-5948-88e4-04282bd52532" :step/math-academy-id 486644 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078571 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078288 :question/id #uuid "b9891d5c-d928-589e-a461-3f0b39fb1b66" :question/math-academy-id "e-4168"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50fb047f-8c8d-56a6-9093-29a68cd89a90" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550004}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing Sums Using Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "2a86dd84-9a2e-5761-bb0a-6256c5f07a30" :step/math-academy-id 486645}] :activity/title "Sigma Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078688}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550121} :activity/id #uuid "730efb98-1f2f-5dbd-abfa-6eb2ca40c973" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820 :topic/id #uuid "c2c914b7-9441-5dec-a44d-57eb7fa8577a" :topic/math-academy-id 673} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c61a37-b0ad-558b-a7d1-6120ba4e9368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549739} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d0838d-ba45-55d3-974e-0b53bb87f9c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2ac819b8-8252-5f38-81b6-27fb48d49f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-251017" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{m=2}^{5}\\frac{2m+1}{10}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum takes integer values of $m$ from $2$ to $5.$ So, computing the terms for $m=2,3,4,5$ and then adding them, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{m=2}^{5}\\frac{2m+1}{10} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1}{10}+\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1}{10}+\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1}{10}+\\frac{2\\cdot 5+1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{7}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{11}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+7+9+11}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 16}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1f9dc2e7-7f93-5620-ae72-7c9fa71554d4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323b99ba-09f2-5bc7-9d64-8e7dd6ab6344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}(8n+8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549423} {:answer/id #uuid "448738d9-f7ba-5faa-973c-18695727c554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}8n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549458} {:answer/id #uuid "4941d029-0c96-5c12-a446-5688c75f9081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}(n+8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549466} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee3a55d-76a5-5964-95de-fa24a41c09c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}(8n-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549526} {:answer/id #uuid "add3a4ae-8c07-5039-ba04-4c97679fbc0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}8n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549458} :answer-field/id #uuid "944f2e05-631f-5917-979a-ad00e83fe8a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd5db24a-0334-5fd9-a195-a6f3785eda3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66995" :question/problem "Express $S=8+16+24+⋯+80+88$ in sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$$\n8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,…,\\,80,\\,88.\n$$\n\n are multiples of $8,$ and since $88÷8=11,$ there are $11$ terms in total. \n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=8n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=11.$ Indeed: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =8(1)=8 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =8(2)=16 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =8(3)=24 \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{10} & =8(10)=80 \\\\ {a}_{11} & =8(11)=88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S=8+16+24+⋯+80+88={\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}8n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ba61d36f-7149-5d34-b5ba-7a9fcfac6d9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1d49e9-52a3-5714-bf0d-4a751bb9d80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549373} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6b6978-9f05-5362-abfd-39a4f83c9478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549691} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a83e13-741b-53db-a957-67d0a0178010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549738} {:answer/id #uuid "f25dcb5d-f98e-51be-bfb1-219423934d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549766} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d1d6c4-1fed-5c72-a347-2744da1b1c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549691} :answer-field/id #uuid "fba6865d-0f42-54a0-ae09-88d638e07043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "470997c7-250a-5a72-adc9-b7b406031a9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-5338" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}5\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum takes integer values of $n$ from $1$ to $3.$ So, computing the terms for $n=1,2,3$ and then adding them, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}5\\cdot {2}^{n-1} & =(5\\cdot {2}^{1-1})+(5\\cdot {2}^{2-1})+(5\\cdot {2}^{3-1}) \\\\  & =(5\\cdot {2}^{0})+(5\\cdot {2}^{1})+(5\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 1)+(5\\cdot 2)+(5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =5+10+20 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fffeb167-2231-519c-b231-4f453c6d3104" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550096}}] :activity/title "Sigma Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549307}]}
